SM(303,{"n":"Exodus 22","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Exodus 22.mp3","d":3550.3,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.28,"Okay, now we're gonna get to uh stealing."],[26.56,"Everybody who's ever been caught stealing."],[28.96,"I did once when I was five."],[31.04,"Never caught me again."],[32.16,"Anyway. Now, if a man steals an ox or a sheep and slaughters or sells it, he shall restore five oxen for an ox and four sheep for a sheep."],[48.08,"Now notice it's gonna become important later, mostly in Deuteronomy."],[53.52,"So we're gonna have to put like a pin in it now."],[56.8,"But notice that the reason for stealing is important in terms of these penalties."],[67.45,"So this person here, so we can we'll address other circumstances later, and that's why I'm saying we've got to put a pin in it."],[74.41,"But this person here is stealing it for the purpose of enriching himself."],[81.1,"Either to sell the meat or to just sell right the sheep or the ox and make money."],[89.35,"So this is someone stealing for their own profit."],[94.87,"He has to pay back multiple times over."],[98.81,"What he took."],[101.65,"And so this is an appropriate restitution because the idea is he loses money on the whole deal."],[111.57,"His goal was to get money."],[114.55,"And he ends up losing money."],[121.19,"If a thief is found breaking in at night and is killed, there shall be no guilt for his blood."],[130.71,"So gotta remember we don't have electricity."],[134.57,"We don't have any of this."],[136.25,"Someone's breaking into your house in the middle of the night."],[139.93,"You end up tangling with them."],[142.47,"They die. That's not murder."],[147.2,"Because it's in the dark, you can't see, you don't know what's going on, you don't know who it is, you don't know what their intent is, why they're there."],[154.34,"So it's sort of treated like self-defense."],[159.46,"Verse 2 of chapter 22."],[161.46,"But if the sun rises on the thief, there shall be guilt if his blood is shed."],[168.1,"However, he should make full restitution, for if he has nothing, then he shall be sold for his theft."],[175.7,"Okay. So there's two pieces there."],[178.1,"Number one, the sun's up, meaning it's light."],[182.24,"You can see him, you know what you know what's going on."],[185.46,"You catch him stealing."],[186.82,"You can't just murder a guy because you caught him stealing."],[190.14,"No arrow. So, um, then, you know, you catch a guy stealing from you and you murder him, that's still murder."],[200.31,"Uh but then the other piece is that doesn't mean like the thief walks away like, oh yeah, he got me."],[208.52,"No harm, no foul."],[210.28,"The thief has to make restitution for whatever he was stealing or whatever property he damaged, or whatever."],[217.56,"And as we talked about when we were talking about how slavery worked in the ancient world last time, if he doesn't have anything in order to make restitution, then he has to sell himself into slavery to work off that debt of what he stole."],[239.12,"If the thing stolen from a donkey to a sheep is found alive in his hand, he shall restore double."],[248.96,"So you catch him walking off with your donkey."],[251.92,"He's got to give your donkey back and one more donkey."],[255.92,"Or one more donkey worth of money."],[259.92,"If a man allows his field or vineyard to be grazed upon, but lets loose his animal and it feeds in the field of another man, he shall make restitution from the best of his own field and the best of his own vineyard."],[275.2,"So I have part of my land that I use to graze my livestock, but my livestock wanders off onto somebody else's property and starts eating stuff."],[286.14,"I have to make restitution, and I can't just give you my leftovers or I have to give you from the best of my land, even if what my animals ate wasn't the best of yours."],[300.0,"I still have to give the best of mine."],[307.68,"Verse 5. If fire breaks out and catches in thorns, but sets threshing floors, stacked grain, or a field on fire, he who kindled the fire shall surely make restitution."],[320.48,"Now this isn't just arson"],[327.8,"Obviously it covers arson."],[329.86,"But this also covers just irresponsibility."],[334.51,"I made a campfire, I was doing XYZ, my fire got loose and it damaged your property."],[343.2,"Then I need to make restitution."],[346.72,"What is this?"],[348.48,"That doesn't make much sense."],[351.12,"So, yeah, what's being translated as thorns there, it's trying to refer to like brush, like dead brush."],[358.56,"You know, so like I start a campfire, there's some dead brush around, the fire jumps into the brush and spreads and goes places where I didn't necessarily want it to go."],[371.0,"So the idea is it becomes a brush fire that gets to the fresh floor."],[374.9,"And it goes and it burns some guy's field or it burns his, yeah, my neighbor."],[382.95,"Yeah, let's pause there for a second because there's important neighbor."],[387.51,"So when we're gonna get statements later, like in Leviticus, to love your neighbor."],[394.25,"That's not just an abstract thing."],[398.34,"Especially in our modern day, we think of love in very sentimental terms."],[403.8,"Oh, I just I love everybody."],[405.08,"everybody Except. Like, that's not what the Bible's talking about"],[407.68,"My neighbor's great."],[412.39,"It's nice that you feel that way."],[414.07,"I hope it's sincere."],[415.35,"But that's not what the Bible's talking about."],[417.73,"The Bible's talking about your actual actions."],[422.3,"Your actual actions."],[424.7,"So that has the action means things on the positive side that we'll talk about more later when we get to those verses."],[432.46,"The translation that comes into English through Latin of charity is actually a very good one in terms of connecting it to actual actions toward people."],[442.38,"But there's also a negative sense of it, because I love my neighbor, there's things I'm not going to do."],[449.02,"Like let my animals roam free on his property and destroy his things"],[454.88,"Like start a fire, you know, that is gonna spread and destroy his home."],[462.1,"Or his fields or his right, there are all these things I'm not going to do because I love my neighbor and I care about him, I'm gonna avoid doing irresponsible things that might harm him."],[473.91,"So the idea, the reason why Christ will say, for example, that love your neighbor as yourself summarizes all this stuff we've been reading, is because it does."],[490.17,"If I actually care about other people. then I'm not going to do these kinds of irresponsible, negligent things that might cause them harm"],[498.77,"And if I mess up and I do one of them, I'm going to make it right."],[503.17,"I'm going to repent, I'm going to go to them, I'm going to apologize, and I'm going to make it right with them."],[509.18,"Whatever harm harm I caused."],[511.74,"That's what it means to love them."],[515.42,"And it doesn't matter how you feel about it, really."],[518.3,"Sincerity's super overrated."],[520.22,"It's unimportant. I would much rather live in a world where everybody does the right thing insincerely than where they do horrible things to each other and really mean it."],[532.42,"Like, sincerity, I don't care."],[535.46,"Okay Um, yeah, but so this is a very practical example."],[545.37,"Of starting, you know, starting a fire to do whatever."],[550.01,"Burn garbage. Whatever."],[552.52,"That you take other people into account."],[557.26,"And do things in a responsible way."],[563.98,"Verse 6. If a man gives his neighbor money or goods to keep, but it is stolen out of the man's house, thief shall pay double if he is found."],[576.26,"So you loan me your hedge trimmer."],[581.72,"I've got it in my garage."],[583.32,"Somebody comes by, steals your hedge trimmer out of my garage."],[588.04,"Guy gets caught with the hedge trimmer trying to pawn it."],[591.24,"He has to pay double."],[592.44,"Why? Because he has to pay me for what he stole from me, and he has to pay you because it belonged to you."],[601.05,"That's the idea here."],[603.05,"But if the thief is not found, then the Lord of the house shall be brought before God, and he shall swear he played no wicked part with regard to any of his neighbor's deposit."],[615.37,"So then what does the person need to do if we don't find the thief?"],[621.51,"I guy steals your hedge trimmer, we never find out what happened to it."],[625.83,"I have to go and swear solemnly before God, look, I had nothing to do with it."],[632.31,"It was stolen from me."],[635.05,"And then I don't have to make any restitution because I didn't wrong you."],[648.06,"For any kind of trespass, whether it concerns an ox, a donkey, a sheep, or clothing, or any alleged loss of whatever kind, the cause of both parties shall come before God, and he that is convicted by God shall repay double to his neighbor."],[663.62,"If anyone gives his neighbor a donkey, an ox, a sheep, or any animal to keep, and it dies, is injured, or is taken, but no one knows it, then an oath of God shall be between them both that he is not guilty with regard to the deposit, and the owner of the deposit shall accept this."],[682.82,"Thus no compensation shall be made to him."],[686.5,"So the same kind of thing."],[688.42,"Make an oath before God."],[690.9,"Once that oath is made, the other person has to accept it."],[695.4,"Well, what if he knows the guy's lying?"],[697.8,"Dude just made an oath before God, God's gonna handle that."],[701.57,"That's the idea here."],[703.25,"Remember when it says, Vengeance is mine, says the Lord."],[706.45,"He doesn't say. there will never be consequences"],[706.55,"He doesn't say vengeance is nobody's."],[713.67,"He doesn't say no one will ever judge the earth."],[716.39,"He just says, You're not the one who gets to judge the earth."],[720.61,"God is. God will take care of it."],[722.87,"If the person is lying."],[729.19,"But if, in fact, the deposit is stolen from him, he shall compensate the owner."],[734.55,"But if it is torn to pieces by a beast, he shall bring it as evidence and make no compensation."],[741.29,"So we'll take it to look."],[743.13,"Judge. Here's what's left."],[746.42,"Of the sheep he loaned me, a wolf got to it, as you can see."],[750.18,"That's what happened"],[755.68,"If a man borrows anything from his neighbor and it becomes injured or dies, the owner not being with it, he shall make compensation to the owner."],[764.32,"But if its owner was with it, he shall not make compensation."],[767.84,"If it was hired, it came for its hire."],[772.32,"Okay. So what does that mean?"],[775.58,"So if it was hired is a separate scenario there."],[779.1,"Higher there is basically sort of the equivalent of like if it was leased."],[783.8,"So if I like, if I'm like, I don't buy a bowl from you, say, but I'm like, I need to make more cows."],[792.6,"s. So, and I don't have a bull who' able to breed"],[798.77,"So I'm like, hey, I'll give you X amount of money for you to loan me your bull to come impregnate my cows, and then I'll give you the bull back after a month."],[810.62,"And three weeks into the month, the bull dies under whatever circumstances."],[815.84,"The idea is you keep all the money."],[819.98,"But that's it."],[822.64,"That's the higher part."],[825.12,"Um the first part, uh, if the owner was with it, well, the before that, it's actually the worst before that."],[833.92,"If whether the owner was with it with. it or not, is not like mentally"],[840.17,"It's it's if he was there, if he was around."],[849.41,"You loan me your donkey to ride to the next town, I come back walking, and I'm like, dude, your donkey died."],[862.66,"Then I need to reimburse you for your donkey."],[867.59,"If I live right next door to you, and I'm like, hey, can I use your donkey to pull this thing out of a hole and you're standing there with me and the donkey keels over and dies, I don't have to pay you for the donkey."],[885.52,"Because you were right there."],[887.06,"Meaning it wasn't really in my custody."],[889.54,"That's the idea."],[893.58,"If you give something over to me in my custody and something happens to it, I'm responsible."],[899.26,"If you and I are both there and something happens to your donkey, that's not my fault, per se."],[908.0,"That's what that's getting at."],[910.13,"Um And as I said, all of these things are these very, I know this stuff gets a little tedious."],[918.69,"R?ight All of these possible cases, of things"],[922.51,"But again, these little things, are what love of neighbor looks like."],[930.78,"When it's actually applied in the real world."],[934.88,"If you lend me something, how I treat that thing you lent me reflects my view of you, how I feel about you."],[946.74,"You lend me something and I treat it like garbage and give it back to you, you know, beaten up and trashed."],[952.84,"That says something."],[956.62,"About what I think of you."],[958.47,"You lend me something and I give it back to you. you in as good or even better shape than let it to me"],[965.23,"That says something about what I think of you, the respect I have for you."],[972.56,"What love actually means is made up of all of these little things in the real world."],[982.48,"How we conduct ourselves and these rudimentary little things day to day."],[986.4,"All the little decisions we make all day, every day."],[993.44,"Okay. know where You you know we're getting into an interesting part when uh they can't even come up with a good subheading."],[1006.2,"Oral laws. Before that, like stolen property."],[1011.24,"Well, that's pretty on point."],[1013.24,"But now they're just like a bunch of bunch of stuff."],[1017.56,"My wife got mad at me for that when we moved here."],[1021.0,"Uh, because like half the boxes."],[1025.56,"I know a good chunk of you weren't here to actually help us move in."],[1030.2,"A couple of you were you."],[1032.84,"Okay. Arthur, I think you might have known."],[1035.32,"No, I didn't."],[1036.2,"Nobody actually helped us unload the boxes."],[1037.96,"Okay. Maybe it might have."],[1039.24,"It was seven years ago now."],[1040.44,"Yeah, it's probably somebody from your family was probably there."],[1043.0,"Uh so like the boxes she labeled were very specific."],[1046.94,"It was like bedroom dresser, this."],[1048.06,"Like very detailed"],[1050.71,"I had labels like stuff, junk, more stuff."],[1057.27,"Occasionally she'd be happy because it would say like bedroom stuff, and she'd like, okay, well, at least I know which, you know."],[1063.91,"That's the kind of subheading we had stuff."],[1067.75,"Bunch of things."],[1070.55,"Okay. Verse 15."],[1073.11,"If a man entices a virgin who is not betrothed and lies with her, he shall surely pay the bride price for her to be his wife."],[1084.63,"Entices is a nice nice euphemism there."],[1087.83,"Um so this is a woman not betrothed to anybody, not. engaged to anybody, not married, obviously"],[1096.67,"And some man comes and seduces her and sleeps with her, takes her virginity, he has to marry her."],[1108.19,"In order to understand this and some of the other things coming up, we have to remember, again, marriage was a very different institution in the ancient world than it is now."],[1118.67,"Okay. In fact, the way we look at marriage now in contemporary society, American society, especially, is weird compared to the rest of the world now and the rest of history."],[1132.61,"In that we have this whole idea of oh, we meet someone and we f.all in love with them, and then we decide to get married and spend the rest of our lives together"],[1142.17,"So for most of the history of the world, you were around between the ages of 13 and 15, you were married to someone."],[1151.37,"Sometimes you had never met them before you were married."],[1156.35,"Sometimes you had, because you were in the same village or whatever, but your parents and their parents arranged it."],[1163.15,"And then you were married, and that was it."],[1167.69,"Until one of you died."],[1169.87,"Or if you were really unlucky and you you were a woman, until he decided to get rid of you."],[1176.8,"Or you died."],[1177.82,"Because men could divorce women very easily."],[1179.58,"The reverse of not true"],[1185.3,"So that was the reality of marriage."],[1188.32,"That's just it."],[1190.56,"That's how it existed."],[1192.24,"And so when we read these commandments governing marriage, we have to realize."],[1198.61,"So, like, this seems horrible to us because we're like, wait, she has to marry him?"],[1204.77,"Yeah. That's how we view it."],[1206.64,"We're looking at it from her."],[1208.16,"Well, wait, what if she just, you know, got talked into sleeping with him and doesn't want to marry him."],[1213.14,"Well, here's the thing."],[1214.54,"In the ancient world, she's no longer a virgin."],[1218.66,"She could not contract a legal marriage."],[1223.82,"If she claimed to be a virgin and was found on her wedding night to not be a virgin, she could be killed."],[1234.14,"In ancient societies."],[1237.26,"So this man, by seducing her, has taken something away from her that he can't give back in restitution in that society."],[1250.67,"Also have to realize this is a society where polygamy exists."],[1255.47,"So this is not saying she and him have to form a nuclear family."],[1259.87,"The nuclear family does not exist as a concept."],[1264.67,"Okay, so this is not like the two of them are gonna get a little house somewhere even though they don't like each other and they have to."],[1271.56,"This is she becomes part of his the big extended family that he's part of."],[1277.24,"Meaning she's gonna be taken care of for the rest of her life."],[1284.65,"Because she can't now go in direct illegal marriage."],[1290.18,"The opposite of this, what would normally happen in most societies is a man would seduce a woman to take her virginity, and she could look forward to a lifespan as a prostitute."],[1301.62,"Or a beggar, but beggars usually died young."],[1306.1,"Female beggars usually prostitute."],[1308.58,"Yes, you end up being, yeah."],[1309.7,"And so rather than that, God says, no, you can't do this"],[1315.23,"Says to men who are the ones making this decision, you can't do that."],[1320.11,"You can't go and seduce a woman and take her virginity and then just walk away and leave her to whatever in society."],[1328.83,"If you go and you take that from her, you you are responsible to take care of her for the rest of her life."],[1336.37,"It's more like our child support legislation in the contemporary world."],[1342.31,"Where if a man gets a woman pregnant."],[1345.21,"She has the baby, the man doesn't matter."],[1348.25,"He's helping provide money for the child until the child's 18."],[1352.73,"We have to think about it more like that"],[1354.46,"It's more like that."],[1357.88,"It's not about the relationship between the two people."],[1360.2,"The two people do not have to have any, they never have to speak again to keep this commandment."],[1366.52,"He just has to make sure she's provided for for the rest of her life."],[1372.06,"Because he took that from her."],[1386.46,"That was actually another form of restitution, is the way it actually worked in the ancient world."],[1390.78,"I'm not saying we should go back to this."],[1392.54,"I'm just saying this is how it works in the ancient world."],[1394.94,"The idea was not like I'm selling my daughter to you."],[1398.5,"Okay? Because, remember, we talked about this last time."],[1402.5,"This is a society where a dad could sell his daughter as a slave."],[1408.02,"Okay. There's a difference between arranging a marriage for your daughter and selling your daughter as a slave."],[1414.98,"These were two different things, both of which men could do in the Bronze Age."],[1421.38,"So these are not the same thing."],[1423.7,"Clearly, in their minds."],[1425.32,"These are very different things."],[1426.76,"The idea of the bride price was not that you're selling her as a slave."],[1431.88,"The idea of the bride price was that you're making restitution to the family for the loss of the daughter."],[1439.96,"Because the daughter leaves that family."],[1440.36,"the And it's now part of husband's family."],[1443.44,"It's no longer part of that family."],[1450.8,"And when you're living in big extended families and you're doing subsistence farming, every set of hands that you have on that farm is incredibly valuable."],[1462.58,"Incredibly valuable. All the work she could do, everything she could contribute to that household, you're giving up for her to be married and enter this other household."],[1472.66,"Who's going to get the benefit of all that?"],[1475.06,"And so paying a dowry."],[1478.59,"Paying this at the time of the marriage was seen as a kind of restitution."],[1486.85,"And if it was considered a great insult to offer a meager amount."],[1496.58,"You're effectively saying, Oh, my wife isn't worth that much."],[1501.56,"So the idea is you would offer an extravagant amount."],[1505.56,"And we're gonna see cases like an infamous case with David, where men who didn't necessarily have that much money would go and offer like crazy things like I'm gonna go and do some wild thing, in order to, you know, and uh in her name or something, as part. of the right the dowry"],[1530.23,"And this is also what happened with remember uh Jacob with Leia and Rachel, he didn't have any money."],[1538.23,"So when he wanted to marry Rachel, he's like, I'll come and work for you for seven years."],[1541.83,"He basically sold himself as a slave to her father for seven years, I'm gonna offer you seven years of my labor, in return for your daughter."],[1556.25,"Okay. But if her father utterly refuses to give her to him, he shall pay money to her father according to the bride price of virgins."],[1566.89,"So if the dad says, No dirt bag, I'm not letting my daughter marry you."],[1573.15,"Even though you seduced her, she can remain in her father's house, and then it's her father's job, to care for her."],[1582.51,"Their father's big household, not just her dad."],[1586.19,"But the household will take care of her for the rest of her life, but he still has to pay what he would have paid if he married her."],[1598.69,"Um to that household, nonetheless."],[1609.62,"Then you could see why we got the stuff heading because immediately after this, you shall not permit a sorcerer to live"],[1618.78,"The King James, I like the old King James."],[1621.66,"You know what that was?"],[1623.1,"Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live."],[1627.9,"Yeah, this translation says woman who practices sorcery."],[1639.02,"I was like, Yeah."],[1641.9,"So the point is the witchcraft, not the biological sex of the person doing it."],[1652.22,"The reason that women were associated with this is women died young from childbirth."],[1659.38,"If you had an old woman, she was kind of supernatural at some point."],[1662.42,"Yeah. Yeah. So she had powers and such."],[1665.46,"Yeah, yeah. But there were also men who did this stuff."],[1667.94,"Yeah. Yeah. Um now when women did it, so as I think we've talked about before, um, and we'll definitely talk about again, um people think that there were priestesses in the ancient world, meaning there were women who were priests, like these pagan cults that did the same thing that male priests did."],[1696.9,"Uh no. That's not a thing that ever happened."],[1703.18,"The people who referred to as priestesses, that's a polite way of saying temple or shrine prostitute, usually."],[1709.5,"They were doing things very different."],[1712.7,"Now, there were plenty of places in the pagan world where they also had male shrine prostitutes."],[1720.27,"But they did not have females who were doing what the male priests did."],[1724.88,"So people who we would think of somewhat anachronistic anachronistically looking back as like a male sorcerer or wizard or whatever the language you want to use, practice practitioner of magic or witchcraft or whatever, who's a male."],[1745.48,"We' usreually. integrated into some pagan cult as a priest or a prophet"],[1751.16,"Think like Balaam, son of Baor as a prophet, uh, or as the priest of some pagan god."],[1758.64,"Whereas a woman who was doing that kind of stuff was doing it outside of those sort of power structures."],[1766.32,"Think like the witch of Endor, who Saul, when he's king, goes to visit."],[1771.3,"She wasn't like a priestess of a particular god or goddess."],[1776.42,"She's outside of those structures."],[1780.26,"She's kind of doing this stuff."],[1782.66,"So that's another piece of, that's sort of, you know, the the prophets of Baal aren't gonna be suffered to live either."],[1789.8,"When Elijah comes around"],[1794.45,"But um, all right, so it it's really about the thing, but there are times where female practitioners are singled out because they're not caught by the other category."],[1806.77,"So you could say stuff about pagan priests and false prophets and stuff, but that doesn't actually cover those female practitioners because they're outside of that structure."],[1816.47,"When you're talking about different groups."],[1820.75,"But notice this is an issue of identity."],[1825.87,"Meaning it's a sorcerer or a witch."],[1830.56,"Or however we want to translate it."],[1833.97,"Meaning this is someone who's doing that on an ongoing basis."],[1840.87,"Who can't be suffered, who can't be allowed to continue to live as that is the idea."],[1850.33,"So this is not if you find somebody doing witchcraft, kill on sight."],[1856.47,"That is not what this is saying."],[1858.23,"This is saying if a person will not stop doing this."],[1863.28,"If they will not stop practicing this, they have to be cut off from among the people."],[1870.12,"So it's the same."],[1871.0,"Maximum penalty being death, but also there could be exile."],[1874.52,"So it's the same kind of logic as Joshua with most of the Canaanites"],[1880.8,"Yeah. Yeah. And you read about, like in the book of Acts, it talks about people coming when St."],[1889.2,"Paul's preaching to non Jewish people."],[1891.86,"To Gentiles. Talks about them coming and burning their texts of sorcery and their magic books and stuff."],[1899.92,"And giving that up."],[1902.1,"So the idea again is of saying you can't go on doing this and live, at least not live here."],[1909.84,"The idea is not that we want to kill people, it's that we don't want people to do this, and if they're doing this, we want them to repent and stop."],[1925.08,"Whoever lies with an animal shall surely be put to death."],[1929.8,"That seems like a non sequitur."],[1933.72,"I will not be graphic in describing why it's not, but I think I can euphemistically describe why it's not."],[1942.2,"Particularly in the Baal cult, as one example."],[1947.64,"Baal worshippers, there was a particular feast on which such acts took place with the uh the priests of Baal."],[1962.52,"Um, so this is. something that was a part of pagan ritual practice"],[1969.84,"That doesn't mean it's only wrong if it's part of pagan ritual practice."],[1976.8,"We're going to see Leviticus 18."],[1979.04,"It's also wrong in general."],[1981.29,"But the reason it's brought up here between sorcery and between what we're about to read is that it was part of pagan ritual practice."],[1995.29,"He who sacrifices to any god except to the Lord alone, he shall be put to death."],[2000.59,"So it's in between sacrificing to other gods and practicing magic."],[2008.33,"That's why it's in between there, because of its connection to pagan ritual."],[2016.73,"You shall neither mistreat a stranger nor oppress him, for you are strangers in the land of Egypt."],[2026.09,"So stranger here is not someone you don't know."],[2029.21,"Stranger here is foreigner, not Israelite."],[2035.87,"When someone you run into someone who's not one of your people."],[2042.72,"You shall not oppress them."],[2046.84,"And what does God point out here?"],[2049.72,"This is what h.appened to you in Egypt"],[2052.56,"You were living in Egypt and you were not Egyptians, and what did they do?"],[2058.06,"You are not to do that."],[2060.8,"You are not to do that."],[2062.88,"Um this is, of course, the flip of offering hospitality to strangers, which we have seen and we'll see again in here."],[2071.84,"Right again. There's the negative and the positive aspects of showing love."],[2076.42,"If you love the stranger, philocinia."],[2079.8,"This is a in Greek, this is the love of the stranger, the love of the foreigner."],[2084.61,"Showing hospitality. There's the positive aspect of here are the things you do for such a person."],[2088.97,"Th'eres the negative aspect of here, the things you don't do to such a person."],[2092.85,"a person. And remember, this is because the foreigner is vulnerable"],[2098.79,"What could the Israelites do about Egypt oppressing them?"],[2103.83,"No. Especially like a lone stranger or one family."],[2110.87,"What could they do?"],[2111.75,"They didn't have any legal rights to anything."],[2118.04,"And so someone could get away with harming them."],[2122.28,"We talked about this when we were talking about how commandments are direct tend to be directed toward men rather than women in a lot of areas."],[2129.97,"Because women didn't have power or authority to make choices in this society."],[2134.07,"And so a lot of it was protecting them from men."],[2137.41,"And what men might want to do to and with them."],[2141.49,"Same thing with your foreigners."],[2143.97,"We're gonna see another category here in a second."],[2147.38,"From the Taurus perspective, what reveals your real character is how you treat people who can't do anything about it."],[2158.66,"Someone you could get away with abusing."],[2162.87,"How do you treat them?"],[2165.27,"That's the real mark of your character."],[2169.43,"We talked about this a little back in the story of Lot and his daughters and the angels who visited in Sodom."],[2176.04,"Which is a story that blows our modern frame of reference."],[2177.88,"But the idea there was these are people to which Lot kind of owed nothing"],[2185.1,"And as we see from the other men of Sodom, they could have done whatever they wanted to if they were just humans and couldn't protect themselves."],[2192.4,"So how Lot chooses to treat them tells us a lot about who Lot is, as opposed to the men of Sodom, who are facing God's judgment."],[2208.46,"Verse 21, you shall not afflict any widow or orphan."],[2212.64,"So here's another two categories."],[2215.69,"Widows don't have a husband, orphans don't have a father, meaning no no one to one to represent them in court, no one to defend their rights, to avenge them if you do something them."],[2231.09,"Notice what God says in verse 22 about all of these groups the stranger."],[2234.95,"Meaning the foreigner, the widow, the orphan."],[2237.67,"If you afflict them in any way and they cry out to me, I will surely hear their cry."],[2246.29,"So God says, Yeah, you're right."],[2248.37,"They don't have somebody who's gonna come get revenge on you."],[2252.85,"They don't have somebody who's gonna take you to court."],[2256.77,"But when they talk to me about it."],[2258.87,"When they talk to God about it, God's gonna hear them."],[2261.79,"And God's gonna take care of it."],[2263.87,"And that should scare you."],[2266.45,"That should scare you."],[2267.81,"Way more than them having a dad or a husband."],[2275.93,"Verse 23 My wrath will become hot."],[2278.65,"I will kill you with the sword."],[2280.57,"Your wives shall be widows, and your children will be orphans."],[2284.81,"That's about as direct as it gets."],[2290.62,"So, and notice the kind of vengeance."],[2295.36,"The kind of justice that's here talking about."],[2298.14,"You oppressed a widow, okay, your wife is going to be a widow."],[2302.7,"You oppressed an orphan, okay, your children will be orphans."],[2306.32,"It's that idea, we'll rebalance the scales."],[2310.76,"For what you did."],[2315.88,"If you lend money to any poor brother among you, you shall not be hard on him or charge him interest."],[2325.8,"So here's a fun one."],[2327.56,"Uh this is why our entire society is built on sin."],[2333.4,"It is a sin to lend money at interest."],[2339.96,"It's a sin starting here in the Torah."],[2342.12,"It was a sin all through the history of Israel."],[2344.44,"It was regarded by the church as a sin."],[2347.48,"Still is, if you read the canons, to this very day."],[2349.24,"It's in there"],[2356.65,"It is a sin."],[2359.13,"It is not true, as some people will say, to try to justify other sins that the church now thinks usury is okay."],[2368.57,"It does not."],[2370.09,"It is still a sin."],[2372.41,"No matter how many people identify as Christians and do it."],[2377.45,"And it doesn't seem to be a degree."],[2379.29,"I've always said it as excessive interest."],[2382.09,"No, well, that's, you see, that's the slight."],[2383.77,"Well, no, you know, you're reasonable interest."],[2386.41,"No. And you know enough medieval history to know that Christians were not allowed to to lend money at interest other Christians, period."],[2396.19,"The Jews did it."],[2397.09,"And that's why other people who weren't Christians got into that business."],[2403.78,"Um because usury is a sin."],[2409.54,"And why is it a sin?"],[2412.34,"Well, because as God points out here, who is it who needs to borrow money?"],[2418.26,"It's the poor."],[2420.42,"Why are you trying to profit from someone else's poverty?"],[2428.42,"That's sick. That's predatory."],[2434.34,"That's predatory. Christ is going to say, you should just give them the money."],[2436.34,"to. You shouldn't even lend it them"],[2442.67,"But a bare minimum, you can't lend it at interest."],[2449.71,"Later on in Ezekiel, God's going to say that he's going to talk about a bunch of people who have lent money and interest."],[2456.91,"Say when they die, their blood will be upon their own hands."],[2460.03,"Like, this is not like low grade sin either."],[2463.55,"This is serious sin in the scriptures, lending money and interest."],[2468.67,"It is always exploitative."],[2471.95,"It is always a sin."],[2473.47,"It is always wrong."],[2476.59,"Wait, when it says that, does it mean like the same like blood guiltiness of like murder is on Ephelius?"],[2481.83,"In Ezekiel? That's the language that's used for like murder, yes."],[2484.95,"Yeah, yeah, yeah."],[2485.43,"So it's the same idea."],[2487.75,"Yes. Yes. It's that you're being predatory on your fellow humans."],[2492.57,"Trying to profit from their poverty."],[2496.06,"This is just baked in here."],[2497.58,"We have to deal with it."],[2500.38,"Doesn't matter that we live in a capitalist country, we have to deal with it."],[2504.35,"That this is just something, and it's baked into our society now, but it's deeply wrong."],[2512.17,"And I've I've said this in front of somebody who I had a long conversation with afterwards."],[2518.45,"The last time, 12 years ago or so when I was going through this, was somebody who was a mortgage broker."],[2523.05,"That's how he made a living"],[2525.73,"Um, and there so there are two there are two competing things here when you get to the practical."],[2533.81,"Oh, let me also add, I had someone uh insist to me uh that oh uh usury in the Byzantine Empire was only forbidden to the clergy."],[2547.75,"And my response to that was yeah, so is divorce."],[2553.03,"Does that mean it's not wrong?"],[2555.65,"Does that mean it's not sinful or bad because it was things that are forbidden to the clergy are things that are sinful and bad."],[2565.65,"Not things that are okay"],[2571.85,"But so there's there's in terms of the practical, so what do we do today?"],[2577.5,"We don't water down the initial statement."],[2581.5,"The lending of money and interest is a sin."],[2584.22,"Always. But practically, how do I live my life in a world that is literally built on usury?"],[2594.46,"Like it is the basic building block of everything I have to deal with every day in my life."],[2601.98,"Number one is we as Christians try to avoid practicing it."],[2605.1,"avoid practicing it. And I, as an individual, could very easily"],[2611.68,"I cannot loan money at interest."],[2616.08,"I could go further like Christ says, I cannot loan money."],[2619.84,"I can just give somebody help if I can, and if I can't, I can't."],[2626.68,"So that part."],[2628.73,"If you're a mortgage broker, that's a little harder."],[2631.53,"So I'll share what I said to the mortgage broker ultimately."],[2634.09,"What I said to the mortgage broker ultimately was: look, you can't buy a house in the United States today, realistically."],[2644.27,"For 99% of the people of the United States."],[2646.67,"You can't buy a house without financing."],[2649.79,"You're gonna have to borrow money at interest to buy a house. in the United States today"],[2654.37,"We can try and overhaul the whole system and make it more Christian at some point in the future."],[2660.53,"May it come soon."],[2661.81,"I think our Lord will return before that happens, but who knows?"],[2666.45,"Who knows what the future holds."],[2668.93,"We're all gonna live our lives in this system where no one's gonna be able to buy a house without borrowing money, and no one's gonna lend them that money without charging them interest, even though it's a sin."],[2681.89,"So I said, you can operate as a mortgage broker in one of two ways."],[2689.27,"You can, within a flawed and even sinful system, a system that's tainted by sin, you can do everything. you can to try to help people get housing that they need"],[2701.95,"And get it in a way that's affordable to them."],[2705.16,"Get housing that's reasonable for them given their financial abilities."],[2709.8,"You can operate that way, or you can operate in a predatory way, where you're trying to maximize your own bonuses and profits from issuing mortgages, where you're writing people mortgages you know they're not going to be able to pay, but you get some benefit from it."],[2724.14,"Etc. etc. etc."],[2725.42,"You can operate that way."],[2726.38,"If you operate that second way, you are deliberately participating in the sin of usury."],[2732.16,"If you operate in the first way, you are doing the best you can to navigate a sinful system, which we all have to do every day."],[2742.92,"It is important on one hand that we are very clear on what is and isn't sin."],[2751.16,"That is very important."],[2753.08,"It is also very important."],[2756.92,"It is also very important that we realize that in the world we live in, in the fallen world we live in, in the sinful world we live in, we often have to make choices where there's no pure option."],[2773.21,"Where there's not an option where we can be completely pure from any."],[2779.79,"Should we run up a ton of credit card debt?"],[2782.11,"No. that's bad and sinful and wrong and a sign of greed"],[2787.27,"If I need to run up a credit card to feed my family, I'm gonna run up a credit card."],[2795.85,"So there are gonna be situations in life, where we have to do things that are not, don't allow us to remain pure."],[2805.43,"We live in a sinful world."],[2806.95,"There's no pure, good, perfectly good option available to us."],[2811.19,"We have to do the best we can."],[2813.59,"We live a life within that of repentance."],[2817.19,"We acknowledge that what we're doing is not ideal and perfect."],[2823.85,"And we dip our toes into the sinful part as little as possible."],[2830.23,"But this is this is an issue."],[2832.63,"This is an issue that most people don't understand."],[2836.87,"They go to one extreme or the other."],[2839.43,"Especially within Christian circles."],[2841.59,"They either go to the one extreme of, look, we've got to live in the quote unquote the real world."],[2847.75,"And so just forget about what is and isn't sin."],[2850.87,"And just try and be nice."],[2854.52,"And be sort of generally nice and kind to everybody, but do whatever."],[2860.86,"Forget about what's sin and what's not."],[2862.62,"Forget about what's all that."],[2864.99,"Um. Or there are people on the other side. who are just like, no, zero compromise, X is sin, X is not"],[2875.27,"And are pretty much unable to live in the real world."],[2878.71,"So most of the people on that side now live online."],[2881.19,"They don't live in the real world."],[2882.87,"They live on the internet where you can just sort of judge everybody by your lofty standards that you're very clear about."],[2890.55,"But you don't actually live your life, so you don't actually have to make any choices in the real world or interact with any actual humans in reality, which would require you to make compromises."],[2900.87,"And the place where we need to be is somewhere in between there"],[2905.05,"Where we're very clear about what is sin and what isn't."],[2907.85,"We're very clear about what's right and wrong."],[2909.89,"But where we also treat people with kindness. and compassion"],[2916.15,"And realize that I and everyone else were doing the best we can to navigate a sinful world."],[2923.4,"And to try and do that with humility and repentance."],[2930.2,"So when we talk about this and when we come back to it later, I'm not going to give this whole spiel again later, but every time we come back to it, because this isn't the last time we're going to see usury declared to be a serious sin."],[2943.14,"We need to be clear about that because in our modern world here in the United States at least, I know people listen to the recording other places."],[2951.7,"So your mileage may vary."],[2953.14,"But here in the United States at least, us.ury is so built into our society that it seems absurd to call it a sin"],[2963.26,"And so that's why I tend to hammer on that when we go through, because the things that, the things that we know that we would all agree, everyone in this room, and probably everyone who comes to this church on Sunday, that we would all agree are bad, like say murder a chapter ago."],[2979.06,"I don't need to hammer on that."],[2980.5,"We all know murder bad."],[2982.12,"Don't go murder people."],[2985.52,"But most of us don't know, and some of us probably don't even think that lending money at interest is bad."],[2993.76,"The scriptures say it is."],[2996.0,"So we need to come back to that."],[2997.52,"That needs to be emphasized. to us"],[3000.84,"The most important passages of scripture are the passages that rub you the wrong way."],[3007.24,"The passages that you don't want to be true."],[3010.04,"The passages that you want to find another way to interpret to not mean what they seem to mean."],[3015.37,"Because you don't like them."],[3017.29,"Those are the most important passages of scripture for you."],[3020.49,"And for me."],[3022.09,"And which ones those are will change at different points in your life."],[3025.87,"There are some that will run you the wrong rub you the wrong way at one point, and then later on you'll be like, no, that's that's right, because you'll get a little older and a little wiser."],[3033.07,"But then there'll be some other passages where you're like, that doesn't mean me."],[3037.84,"That means some other people with some other problems, not me."],[3041.51,"So this is one of those passages that rubs us the wrong way."],[3046.71,"And so that's why it gets some focus."],[3053.11,"Verse 25 If you take your neighbor's garment as a pledge, you shall return it to him before the sun goes down."],[3060.07,"For that is his only covering, his only garment for covering his nakedness."],[3064.07,"What will he sleep in?"],[3065.99,"So it will be when he cries to me, I will hear him, for I am merciful."],[3070.47,"This seems awfully particular, but it's giving a broader thing."],[3075.13,"But it says, take a pledge as a pledge that's like collateral."],[3078.81,"This is still talking about lending money."],[3082.11,"So this is another thing people would do instead of charging interest."],[3085.03,"I want to make sure you're going to pay me back."],[3088.23,"So you give me whatever you have."],[3089.99,"So this is talking about some poor person, all he owns is his cloak."],[3095.19,"So you say, okay, give me that."],[3097.51,"And I'm going to keep it until you pay me back."],[3102.29,"And so God is saying, this is another, we talked about this last time."],[3105.33,"This is another little bit of sarcasm."],[3107.59,"What's he gonna wear to sleep in?"],[3109.75,"You better return that to him before night, because otherwise, what will he sleep in?"],[3112.95,"How will he stay warm?"],[3116.53,"But what's the point of this example, this kind of sarcastic example?"],[3121.34,"From your perspective, you're like, oh, I want to make sure he pays me back."],[3125.1,"So you're not thinking about helping him."],[3129.2,"Again, this isn't you're giving him some money because he's poor and he needs help, then you're helping him."],[3137.6,"It's showing you don't care about him."],[3140.16,"Showing you don't love him."],[3146.24,"As Christ says, even the Gentiles give, expecting as much in return."],[3152.88,"Expecting to get paid back."],[3155.84,"Verse 27, you shall not speak against gods, nor curse a ruler of your people."],[3161.36,"I like that they actually translated that correctly and didn't hide it, because that's what it says."],[3167.46,"It says, You shall not speak against gods. uh, nor curse a ruler of your people"],[3175.1,"Now remember what we said, though, about the slaves last time, bringing them before the gods, before the doorpost."],[3182.78,"Who are the gods they're talking about?"],[3186.2,"They're talking about your ancestors, your forebearers, those above you."],[3192.3,"That's why it's set parallel with the rulers of the people."],[3197.18,"These are people in your family who are in authority over you, the people in the broader structures, the tribe, the nation who are in authority over you."],[3207.16,"You are not to curse them."],[3209.7,"They've been put in authority over you."],[3212.9,"If there was a death penalty for that in the United States, we would all be dead"],[3220.78,"Because we all said something about some president."],[3223.34,"Just saying. Verse 28, you shall not delay to offer the first of your threshing floor and fruit press."],[3234.94,"The firstborn of your sons you shall give to me, likewise you shall do with your calf, sheep, and donkey."],[3240.46,"It shall be with its mother seven days."],[3242.3,"On the eighth day you shall give it to me."],[3244.94,"Now you okay, so pause there."],[3248.08,"Remember, this is just reiterating the dedication of the first fruits."],[3253.44,"The firstborn animals go to God."],[3256.08,"The important thing here, the element that's being added here is there's a command to do it has already been given."],[3263.32,"The element here is not delaying."],[3267.22,"Why would you not delay?"],[3268.74,"Well, the whole point, for example, of giving the first fruits of your harvest, and offering it to God, is that this requires you to have trust in God."],[3279.57,"That the rest of the harvest is going to come."],[3282.87,"So there's a temptation to say, oh yeah, I'll give God the first fruits."],[3286.15,"I'll just keep them here just in case."],[3288.79,"Okay, the rest of the crops coming in."],[3290.71,"Okay, now go give those to God."],[3292.47,"Kind of defeats the purpose of the whole trusting in God thing."],[3298.01,"When you're you're hedging your bets like that."],[3306.45,"And finally, for tonight, verse 30."],[3308.85,"Now you shall be holy men to me, and you shall not eat meat torn by wild animals in the field."],[3314.05,"You shall throw it to the dog."],[3317.17,"This is all of my dog's life verse."],[3320.61,"Um We were talking a second about how this is related."],[3330.79,"This is saying you're not a scavenger."],[3333.21,"What eats an animal that's laying dead somewhere?"],[3336.41,"The buzzard or buzzards, vultures, jackals."],[3342.71,"Yeah. All kinds of really unclean animals."],[3347.25,"Who come and picket dead things."],[3350.23,"Picket corpses. Maggots."],[3352.88,"Yeah. So God says you're not to be like that."],[3360.82,"You find an animal dead, you give the meat to your dogs."],[3365.35,"But you don't eat it."],[3369.0,"This is actually a summary of this whole section we've been reading."],[3376.12,"The section that didn't have a good subheading."],[3379.42,"Because this is talking about overall how you behave."],[3386.58,"You don't behave like a predator, you don't behave like a scavenger."],[3391.88,"You be a holy man."],[3394.2,"You are a human."],[3396.6,"You are created in God's image."],[3401.37,"God is not a scavenger."],[3405.29,"God is not a predator."],[3409.13,"And so that's not how he treats us."],[3412.99,"And so if we're in God's image, we're humans, that's not how we treat each other."],[3423.47,"God did not put other people in the world for my advantage."],[3427.99,"For me to take advantage of them."],[3433.99,"When bad things happen to other people, poverty, whatever other misfortune befalls them, that is not so that I can come and pick."],[3443.43,"So that I can find ways to profit from it."],[3449.99,"Quite the opposite."],[3455.59,"And so to summarize what we've been reading, God picks this very distasteful image."],[3463.91,"To say, when you violate these commandments I've given you, this is what you are like."],[3468.71,"You're like a person who's walking down the road and sees a dead animal and goes and starts eating."],[3476.03,"Quite distasteful. That's what you're like."],[3480.86,"You're lower than dogs."],[3483.74,"When you behave this way."],[3485.74,"Don't behave this way."],[3487.18,"Behave like a human."],[3491.26,"Not just any human, but a human who's holy and who's set apart."],[3496.8,"Because especially with the Israelites, they're not they're set apart from other humans."],[3500.16,"They're called to a higher level even than general humans."],[3506.66,"That means you look at other people in a different way."],[3512.74,"This is the opposite of the Good Samaritan"],[3517.82,"Right in this verse."],[3520.78,"So that happy note."],[3523.42,"That's where we'll wrap it up for tonight."],[3525.18,"Thank you, everybody."],[3527.9,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3535.26,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3540.62,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith."]],"o":[{"t":21.28,"lv":2,"h":"Exodus 22:1 - Theft of ox or sheep","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jokes: caught stealing once at <b>five</b> - \"never caught me again\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Steals ox or sheep, slaughters or sells → restores <b>five oxen for an ox, four sheep for a sheep</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pin in it</b> - motive matters for penalties; more in <b>Deuteronomy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">This thief steals <b>for profit</b> - sell meat or the animal</li><li class=\"d0\">Pays back <b>multiple times over</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Appropriate restitution: goal was money → he <b>loses money</b> on the whole deal</li></ul>"},{"t":121.19,"lv":3,"h":"Night thief vs. daytime thief","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Thief <b>breaking in at night</b>, killed → <b>no guilt for his blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no electricity; dark, can't see who or why → \"not murder\"</li><li class=\"d1\">treated like <b>self-defense</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sun has risen</b> on the thief → guilt if his blood shed</li><li class=\"d1\">light = you can see; can't \"murder a guy because you caught him stealing\" - \"no arrow\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Thief still owes <b>restitution</b> for property taken or damaged</li><li class=\"d1\">no harm, no foul? No</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't pay → <b>sold into slavery</b> to work off the debt (as in last time's slavery discussion)</li><li class=\"d0\">Found alive in his hand, donkey to sheep → <b>restore double</b></li><li class=\"d1\">walk off with your donkey → give it back <b>plus one more donkey</b> (or its worth)</li></ul>"},{"t":259.92,"lv":2,"h":"22:5 - Livestock grazing in another's field","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Man lets animal graze his own field, it wanders into <b>another man's field</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Restitution from the <b>best of his own</b> field and vineyard</li><li class=\"d1\">can't give leftovers</li><li class=\"d1\">even if what animals ate wasn't the best of yours, <b>I give the best of mine</b></li></ul>"},{"t":307.68,"lv":2,"h":"22:5-6 - Fire that spreads","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Fire catches in <b>thorns</b>, burns threshing floors, stacked grain, field → <b>he who kindled it pays</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Covers <b>arson</b> but also plain <b>irresponsibility</b></li><li class=\"d1\">campfire gets loose, damages your property → restitution</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Thorns\" = <b>brush</b>, dead brush</li><li class=\"d1\">fire jumps into the brush, spreads where I didn't want it, burns the neighbor's field</li></ul>"},{"t":382.95,"lv":3,"h":"Love of neighbor is action","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pause: <b>neighbor</b> - later in <b>Leviticus</b>, \"love your neighbor\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not abstract; modern love is <b>sentimental</b>: \"I love everybody... except\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"My neighbor's great\" - nice if sincere, but Bible is talking about <b>actual actions</b></li><li class=\"d0\">English <b>charity</b> (through Latin) connects well to <b>actions toward people</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Negative sense too: because I love him, <b>things I won't do</b></li><li class=\"d1\">let animals roam on his land, start a fire that burns his home or fields</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Christ says <b>love your neighbor as yourself</b> summarizes all this - it does</li><li class=\"d0\">If I care, I avoid <b>irresponsible, negligent</b> things</li><li class=\"d0\">If I mess up: <b>repent, go to them, apologize, make it right</b> - \"that's what it means to love them\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Sincerity's super overrated\"</b> - prefers everyone doing right insincerely to horrors done sincerely</li><li class=\"d0\">Practical example: burning garbage, taking others <b>into account</b></li></ul>"},{"t":563.98,"lv":2,"h":"22:6-11 - Deposits left for safekeeping","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Man gives neighbor money or goods to keep, <b>stolen from the house</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>hedge trimmer</b> loaned to me, stolen from my garage; thief caught pawning it</li><li class=\"d1\">pays <b>double</b>: to me for what he took, to you because it was yours</li><li class=\"d0\">Thief not found → keeper brought <b>before God</b>, swears he <b>played no wicked part</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>no restitution</b> - \"I didn't wrong you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Any trespass: ox, donkey, sheep, clothing, alleged loss → cause of both parties <b>before God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">convicted <b>by God</b> repays double</li><li class=\"d0\">Animal in keeping dies, injured, taken, no one knows → <b>oath of God</b> between them</li><li class=\"d1\">owner <b>must accept</b> it; no compensation</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: what if he knows the guy's lying? → \"God's gonna handle that\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"Vengeance is mine\"</b> - doesn't say vengeance is nobody's, or that no one judges the earth</li><li class=\"d1\">\"You're not the one who gets to judge the earth. <b>God is.</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Deposit <b>stolen</b> → keeper compensates owner</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Torn by a beast</b> → bring remains as evidence, no compensation: \"Judge, here's what's left of the sheep\"</li></ul>"},{"t":755.68,"lv":3,"h":"Borrowed animals; owner present; hired","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Borrow from neighbor, injured or dies, <b>owner not with it</b> → compensate</li><li class=\"d0\">Owner <b>was with it</b> → no compensation</li><li class=\"d1\">loan donkey to ride to next town, dies → I <b>reimburse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you're standing right there when it keels over → not in my <b>custody</b>, no payment</li><li class=\"d1\">\"If you give something into my custody and something happens, <b>I'm responsible</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"If it was hired, it came for its hire\" - <b>hired</b> = like a lease</li><li class=\"d1\">rent your <b>bull</b> for a month to breed cows; dies week three → <b>you keep all the money</b>, that's it</li></ul>"},{"t":910.13,"lv":3,"h":"Love of neighbor in little things","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Admits it gets <b>tedious</b> - all these possible cases</li><li class=\"d0\">These little things are what <b>love of neighbor looks like applied in the real world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How I treat what you lent me reflects <b>my view of you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">return it trashed → says something about what I think of you</li><li class=\"d1\">return it in <b>as good or better shape</b> → says respect</li><li class=\"d0\">Love is made of <b>small day-to-day decisions</b>, \"all day, every day\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Next part: even translators' <b>subheading</b> gives up - \"stolen property\" was on point, now just \"a bunch of stuff\"</li><li class=\"d1\">aside: moving boxes - wife labeled <b>\"bedroom dresser\"</b>; his said <b>\"stuff, junk, more stuff\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1070.55,"lv":2,"h":"22:15 - Seducing a virgin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Man <b>entices</b> a virgin not betrothed and lies with her → pays <b>bride price</b>, she becomes his wife</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Entices\" = nice euphemism; not engaged, not married; he <b>seduces</b> her, takes virginity → must <b>marry her</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1108.19,"lv":3,"h":"Marriage in the ancient world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Marriage very different then; <b>our view is weird</b> historically and globally</li><li class=\"d1\">we meet, fall in love, decide to marry, spend life together</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of history: <b>age 13-15</b>, married</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes never met; sometimes same village; <b>parents arranged it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">until one died - or, if you were a woman, until he <b>got rid of you</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Men could <b>divorce women easily</b>; not the reverse</li><li class=\"d0\">Seems horrible to us: \"she has to marry him?\" - we view it from <b>her</b> side</li></ul>"},{"t":1214.54,"lv":3,"h":"Why this protects her","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In the ancient world she's <b>no longer a virgin</b> → couldn't contract a legal marriage</li><li class=\"d1\">claimed virgin, found not on wedding night → <b>could be killed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">He took something he <b>can't give back</b> in restitution</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Polygamy</b> exists - not a nuclear family; <b>nuclear family not a concept</b></li><li class=\"d1\">she joins his <b>big extended family</b>, taken care of for life</li><li class=\"d0\">Normally: seduced woman faces life as <b>prostitute</b> or <b>beggar</b> (usually died young; female beggars = prostitutes)</li><li class=\"d0\">God to the men: <b>you can't seduce, take virginity, and walk away</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Like our <b>child support</b> law: he provides until the child is 18</li><li class=\"d1\">never have to speak again; he just makes sure she's <b>provided for for life</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1386.46,"lv":3,"h":"Bride price as restitution","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not endorsing a return - \"this is how it works in the ancient world\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Marrying off a daughter ≠ selling her as a slave</b> - dad could do either in Bronze Age</li><li class=\"d0\">Bride price = <b>restitution to the family</b> for loss of the daughter</li><li class=\"d1\">she joins husband's family; <b>subsistence farming</b> - every pair of hands is valuable</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Who's going to get the benefit of all that?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Meager offer = <b>great insult</b> (\"my wife isn't worth that much\") → offer extravagant amounts</li><li class=\"d1\">David and the infamous case; wild things offered in her name</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jacob, Leah and Rachel</b>: no money → <b>seven years' labor</b> for her father</li></ul>"},{"t":1556.25,"lv":3,"h":"If her father refuses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Father <b>utterly refuses</b> → he still pays money <b>according to the bride price of virgins</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"No, dirtbag, I'm not letting my daughter marry you\" → she stays in father's house</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>whole household</b> (not just dad) cares for her for life</li><li class=\"d0\">Still pays what he would have paid to <b>that household</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1609.62,"lv":2,"h":"22:17 - Sorcerer shall not live","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why the \"stuff\" heading: right after, <b>\"you shall not permit a sorcerer to live\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>KJV</b>: \"Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live\"; this translation: <b>woman who practices sorcery</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Point is the <b>witchcraft</b>, not the sex of the practitioner</li><li class=\"d0\">Women associated: died young in childbirth; <b>old woman</b> seemed supernatural</li><li class=\"d1\">but men did this too</li></ul>"},{"t":1667.94,"lv":3,"h":"Priestesses and outside practitioners","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Myth: <b>priestesses</b> doing what male priests did in pagan cults - \"that's not a thing that ever happened\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"priestess\" = polite for <b>temple or shrine prostitute</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pagan world also had <b>male shrine prostitutes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Male \"sorcerer/wizard\" → integrated into pagan cult as <b>priest or prophet</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Balaam son of Beor</b>; priest of a pagan god</li><li class=\"d0\">Woman doing it → <b>outside</b> those power structures</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>witch of Endor</b> whom Saul visits; not priestess of any god</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prophets of Baal</b> also not suffered to live (Elijah)</li><li class=\"d0\">Women singled out because <b>not caught by the other category</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1820.75,"lv":3,"h":"An identity, not a single act","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Issue of <b>identity</b>: a sorcerer or witch <b>on an ongoing basis</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"kill on sight\" - if a person <b>will not stop</b> → <b>cut off from among the people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">max penalty death, but also <b>exile</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same logic as <b>Joshua</b> with most of the Canaanites</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts</b>: Gentiles under Paul <b>burn their books of sorcery</b>, give it up</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: \"can't go on doing this and live - at least not live here\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal not killing people - want them to <b>repent and stop</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1925.08,"lv":2,"h":"22:18-19 - Bestiality and sacrificing to other gods","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Whoever lies with an animal shall surely be put to death\" - seems a <b>non sequitur</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Won't be graphic; part of <b>pagan ritual practice</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Baal</b> cult: a particular feast with the priests of Baal</li><li class=\"d0\">Not only wrong when ritual - also wrong generally (<b>Leviticus 18</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Placed here because of its <b>link to pagan ritual</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"He who sacrifices to any god except the Lord alone\" → put to death</li><li class=\"d1\">so it sits <b>between sacrificing to other gods and practicing magic</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2016.73,"lv":2,"h":"22:20 - The stranger","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Neither mistreat a stranger nor oppress him, for you were strangers in <b>Egypt</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Stranger</b> = <b>foreigner</b>, not Israelite, not someone you don't know</li><li class=\"d0\">God's point: \"this is what happened to you in Egypt\" - <b>don't do that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Flip side of <b>hospitality to strangers</b>; negative and positive aspects of love</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <b>philoxenia</b> = love of the stranger</li><li class=\"d0\">Foreigner is <b>vulnerable</b>: Israelites in Egypt had no legal rights; a lone stranger or one family could be harmed</li></ul>"},{"t":2122.28,"lv":3,"h":"Character shown toward the powerless","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Commands directed at <b>men</b> because women lacked power - protecting them from what men might do</li><li class=\"d0\">Same with foreigners</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Real character</b> = how you treat people who <b>can't do anything about it</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Lot, his daughters, the angels</b> in Sodom</li><li class=\"d1\">Lot owed them nothing; men of Sodom could have done anything to mere humans</li><li class=\"d1\">how Lot treats them shows who he is vs. men facing God's judgment</li></ul>"},{"t":2208.46,"lv":2,"h":"22:21-24 - Widow and orphan","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"You shall not afflict any widow or orphan\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Widow has no <b>husband</b>, orphan no <b>father</b> → no one to represent in court, defend rights, <b>avenge</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Verse 22: afflict them, they cry out → \"I will surely <b>hear</b> their cry\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no one will take you to court - but <b>they talk to God</b>, he takes care of it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"That should scare you way more than them having a dad or a husband\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Verse 23: wrath hot, <b>kill you with the sword</b>; wives widows, children orphans</li><li class=\"d0\">Kind of justice: <b>rebalance the scales</b></li><li class=\"d1\">oppress a widow → your wife becomes one; oppress an orphan → your children</li></ul>"},{"t":2315.88,"lv":2,"h":"22:24 - Lending at interest","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lend to a <b>poor brother</b> → not hard on him or <b>charge interest</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Why our entire society is built on sin\" - <b>usury is a sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sin in the <b>Torah</b>, all through Israel's history, regarded by the <b>church</b> as sin; still is in the <b>canons</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not true that church now thinks usury is okay; \"no matter how many people identify as Christians and do it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Excessive interest\" / \"reasonable interest\" - <b>no</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Medieval: Christians couldn't lend at interest to Christians → <b>Jews</b> did, so others got into that business</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Why</b>: who needs to borrow? <b>The poor</b> - profiting from someone's poverty is <b>predatory</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: <b>just give it</b>, don't even lend; bare minimum: no interest</li></ul>"},{"t":2473.47,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Ezekiel and blood guilt","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ezekiel</b>: those who lent at interest - their <b>blood on their own hands</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not low-grade sin: <b>serious sin</b> in the scriptures</li><li class=\"d0\">It is <b>always exploitative, always a sin, always wrong</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: same <b>blood guiltiness</b> as murder? → yes, language used for <b>murder</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"being predatory on your fellow humans, trying to profit from their poverty\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Baked into our society, \"doesn't matter that we live in a capitalist country\" - but <b>deeply wrong</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2512.17,"lv":2,"h":"Living in a world built on usury","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Said this before to a <b>mortgage broker</b> - long conversation afterward</li><li class=\"d0\">Objection: usury in <b>Byzantium</b> only forbidden to clergy</li><li class=\"d1\">\"So is divorce\" - things forbidden to clergy are <b>sinful</b>, not okay</li><li class=\"d0\">Don't <b>water down</b> the statement: lending at interest is <b>always</b> sin</li><li class=\"d0\">Practical: usury is \"the basic building block of everything I deal with\"</li><li class=\"d1\">#1 <b>avoid practicing it</b>; I can lend nothing at interest, even <b>just give help</b></li><li class=\"d0\">To the broker: <b>99%</b> can't buy a house without <b>financing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">may overhaul the system someday - \"I think our Lord will return before that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two ways to operate:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>help people get affordable housing</b> reasonable for their means - navigating a <b>sinful system</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>predatory</b>: maximize bonuses, write mortgages you know they can't pay → <b>deliberately participating</b> in usury</li></ul>"},{"t":2742.92,"lv":3,"h":"No pure option in a fallen world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Be <b>very clear</b> what is and isn't sin - and realize often <b>no pure option</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Credit card</b> debt out of greed: bad. Run up a card to <b>feed my family</b>: I will</li><li class=\"d0\">Live a life of <b>repentance</b>; acknowledge not ideal; \"dip our toes into the sinful part as little as possible\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2830.23,"lv":3,"h":"Two extremes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Most people go to one extreme, esp. in Christian circles</li><li class=\"d1\">\"real world\" - <b>forget what's sin</b>, just be nice</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>zero compromise</b> - unable to live in the real world; <b>live online</b>, judge by lofty standards, never make choices or deal with real humans</li><li class=\"d0\">Where we need to be: <b>clear on sin</b> and right and wrong, but <b>kindness and compassion</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Everyone doing their best in a sinful world → <b>humility and repentance</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2930.2,"lv":2,"h":"Why he hammers on usury","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Won't repeat the spiel; not the last time usury is declared a <b>serious sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In the <b>US</b>, usury so built in it seems <b>absurd to call a sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Everyone agrees <b>murder</b> is bad - no need to hammer; many don't think <b>lending at interest</b> is bad</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Most important passages</b> = those that <b>rub you the wrong way</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the ones you want to interpret away</li><li class=\"d1\">which ones change over time; some \"that means some other people, not me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">This is one of those passages → gets focus</li></ul>"},{"t":3053.11,"lv":2,"h":"22:25-26 - Garment as pledge","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Take neighbor's <b>garment as a pledge</b> → <b>return before sundown</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his only covering, \"what will he sleep in?\" - he cries to me, <b>I will hear him, for I am merciful</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pledge</b> = collateral; still about lending; alternative to charging interest</li><li class=\"d1\">poor man's only possession is his <b>cloak</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Another bit of <b>sarcasm</b> (as last time): what will he wear to sleep in? how stay warm?</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: you're thinking of <b>repayment</b>, not helping him → shows you <b>don't love him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: <b>even the Gentiles give expecting as much in return</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3155.84,"lv":2,"h":"22:27 - Do not curse gods or rulers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"You shall not speak against <b>gods</b>, nor curse a <b>ruler</b> of your people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Likes that they translated it correctly and didn't hide it</li><li class=\"d0\">Gods = <b>ancestors, forebears, those above you</b> (cf. slaves before the doorpost last time)</li><li class=\"d1\">parallel with <b>rulers of the people</b>: family authority, tribe, nation</li><li class=\"d0\">Not to curse those <b>put in authority</b> over you</li><li class=\"d0\">\"If there was a death penalty for that in the US, we'd all be dead\" - said something about some president</li></ul>"},{"t":3223.34,"lv":2,"h":"22:28-29 - First fruits not delayed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Don't delay first of <b>threshing floor and fruit press</b>; <b>firstborn sons</b> to me; likewise calf, sheep, donkey</li><li class=\"d1\">with its mother <b>seven days</b>, eighth day give to me</li><li class=\"d0\">Reiterates <b>dedication of first fruits</b>; command already given</li><li class=\"d0\">New element: <b>not delaying</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Giving first fruits <b>requires trust</b> that the rest of the harvest will come</li><li class=\"d1\">temptation: keep them \"just in case,\" give them once the crop is in</li><li class=\"d1\">\"hedging your bets\" defeats the whole <b>trusting in God</b> point</li></ul>"},{"t":3306.45,"lv":2,"h":"22:30 - No meat torn by beasts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Holy men to me\" - not eat meat <b>torn by wild animals in the field</b>; <b>throw it to the dog</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"My dog's life verse\"</li><li class=\"d0\">You're not a <b>scavenger</b>: what eats dead animals? <b>buzzards, vultures, jackals</b> - unclean animals</li><li class=\"d1\">God says don't be like that; give the meat to dogs</li></ul>"},{"t":3369.0,"lv":2,"h":"Summary: not predator, not scavenger","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Summary of the whole section with no good subheading: <b>how you behave</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not a <b>predator</b>, not a <b>scavenger</b>; <b>be a holy man</b> - human, created in <b>God's image</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God is neither; so that's not how he treats us or how we treat each other</li><li class=\"d0\">God did <b>not put others in the world for my advantage</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Their poverty or misfortune is <b>not</b> for me to <b>profit</b> from</li><li class=\"d0\">Violate these → \"you're like a person who sees a dead animal and starts eating\" - <b>lower than dogs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Behave like a human - <b>holy and set apart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Israelites set apart even from other humans, called to a <b>higher level</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Means you look at others differently - \"the <b>opposite of the Good Samaritan</b>\"</li></ul>"}]});
